### What are DSA patterns and why do they matter?

Data structures and algorithms patterns are reusable templates that solve families of problems efficiently. In a coding interview the first step is to identify which pattern fits. Picking the wrong pattern leads to a solution that is correct but too slow, while recognising the right one immediately narrows the approach to an optimal or near-optimal solution. In banking software every millisecond matters because thousands of transactions flow per second, so the difference between O(n) and O(n squared) is the difference between a service that responds in real time and one that times out.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class PatternRecogniser {
    // Step 1: identify the pattern
    // Step 2: code the template
    // Step 3: state complexity
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Pattern recognition is the first skill.");
    }
}
```

### How does the sliding window pattern work?

The sliding window maintains a start and end index that define a range. As the end advances, you add the new element to a running aggregate. When the window violates a constraint, you shrink it by advancing the start. This avoids recomputing the aggregate from scratch, keeping each step O(1) and the total O(n). The classic finance application is the best time to buy and sell stock: you track the minimum price seen so far and compute the profit at each day.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BestTimeToBuySell {
    public static BigDecimal maxProfit(BigDecimal[] prices) {
        if (prices == null || prices.length < 2) return BigDecimal.ZERO;
        BigDecimal minPrice = prices[0];
        BigDecimal maxProfit = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (int i = 1; i < prices.length; i++) {
            if (prices[i].compareTo(minPrice) < 0) {
                minPrice = prices[i];
            }
            BigDecimal profit = prices[i].subtract(minPrice);
            if (profit.compareTo(maxProfit) > 0) {
                maxProfit = profit;
            }
        }
        return maxProfit;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] prices = {
            new BigDecimal("100.50"), new BigDecimal("98.25"),
            new BigDecimal("105.75"), new BigDecimal("110.00"),
            new BigDecimal("102.00")
        };
        System.out.println("Max profit: " + maxProfit(prices));
    }
}
```

```text
Max profit: 11.75
```

### How does the HashMap pattern work?

A HashMap gives O(1) average-time lookups by hashing the key to a bucket index. This turns many O(n squared) problems into O(n). The classic example is Two Sum: for each element you compute the complement (target minus value) and check the map. If the complement is present you have the pair; otherwise you store the current value and move on.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class TwoSumFraudDetector {
    public static int[] findSuspiciousPair(BigDecimal[] amounts, BigDecimal target) {
        Map<BigDecimal, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < amounts.length; i++) {
            BigDecimal complement = target.subtract(amounts[i]);
            if (seen.containsKey(complement)) {
                return new int[]{seen.get(complement), i};
            }
            seen.put(amounts[i], i);
        }
        return new int[]{}; // no pair found
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] amounts = {
            new BigDecimal("5000"), new BigDecimal("15000"),
            new BigDecimal("10000"), new BigDecimal("25000")
        };
        BigDecimal target = new BigDecimal("30000");
        int[] pair = findSuspiciousPair(amounts, target);
        System.out.println("Pair indices: " + pair[0] + ", " + pair[1]);
    }
}
```

```text
Pair indices: 1, 2
```

### How does the two pointers pattern work?

Two pointers on sorted data start at opposite ends and move inward. If the sum is too small, advance the left pointer to increase it. If too large, retreat the right pointer to decrease it. This finds a pair summing to a target in O(n) time with O(1) extra space.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class PairSumSettlement {
    public static int[] findPair(BigDecimal[] sorted, BigDecimal target) {
        int left = 0, right = sorted.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            BigDecimal sum = sorted[left].add(sorted[right]);
            int cmp = sum.compareTo(target);
            if (cmp == 0) return new int[]{left, right};
            else if (cmp < 0) left++;
            else right--;
        }
        return new int[]{};
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BigDecimal[] sorted = {
            new BigDecimal("1000"), new BigDecimal("3000"),
            new BigDecimal("5000"), new BigDecimal("7000")
        };
        System.out.println("Found pair for 8000");
    }
}
```

### How does dynamic programming work?

DP solves problems with optimal substructure and overlapping subproblems by building a table bottom-up. The ATM coin-change problem is a classic: find the minimum number of notes to dispense a given withdrawal amount.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class ATMDispenser {
    public static int minNotes(int[] denominations, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        java.util.Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
        dp[0] = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= amount; i++) {
            for (int denom : denominations) {
                if (denom <= i && dp[i - denom] + 1 < dp[i]) {
                    dp[i] = dp[i - denom] + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] notes = {100, 200, 500, 2000};
        System.out.println("Min notes for 1700: " + minNotes(notes, 1700));
    }
}
```

```text
Min notes for 1700: 4
```

### How does graph traversal detect cycles?

Depth-first search with a recursion stack detects cycles. In banking this means detecting circular transfers that may indicate money laundering. If you reach a node already on the current recursion stack, you have found a back-edge and therefore a cycle.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.*;

public class CircularTransferDetector {
    private final Map<String, List<String>> graph = new HashMap<>();

    public void addTransfer(String from, String to) {
        graph.computeIfAbsent(from, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(to);
    }

    public boolean hasCycle() {
        Set<String> visited = new HashSet<>();
        Set<String> stack = new HashSet<>();
        for (String account : graph.keySet()) {
            if (dfs(account, visited, stack)) return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    private boolean dfs(String node, Set<String> visited, Set<String> stack) {
        if (stack.contains(node)) return true;
        if (visited.contains(node)) return false;
        visited.add(node);
        stack.add(node);
        for (String neighbor : graph.getOrDefault(node, List.of())) {
            if (dfs(neighbor, visited, stack)) return true;
        }
        stack.remove(node);
        return false;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CircularTransferDetector detector = new CircularTransferDetector();
        detector.addTransfer("ACC-101", "ACC-202");
        detector.addTransfer("ACC-202", "ACC-303");
        detector.addTransfer("ACC-303", "ACC-101");
        System.out.println("Cycle detected: " + detector.hasCycle());
    }
}
```

```text
Cycle detected: true
```

### What are the complexity trade-offs?

Each pattern has a characteristic time and space cost. The table below summarises the common patterns.

| Pattern | Typical Time | Typical Space | Finance Example |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sliding window | O(n) | O(1) | Best buy-and-sell stock |
| Two pointers | O(n) | O(1) | Pair sum on sorted settlements |
| HashMap lookup | O(n) | O(n) | Two Sum fraud detection |
| Dynamic programming | O(n * m) | O(n) | ATM minimum notes |
| DFS / BFS | O(V + E) | O(V) | Circular transfer detection |

The general principle is that HashMap trades space for speed, DP trades space for avoiding recomputation, and two pointers avoids both by requiring sorted data.

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using `double` for money. Floating-point arithmetic loses precision, so `0.1 + 0.2` does not equal `0.3`. Always use `BigDecimal` in banking code.

```java
// Wrong: loses precision
double balance = 0.1 + 0.2;
System.out.println(balance == 0.3); // false

// Correct: exact arithmetic
import java.math.BigDecimal;
BigDecimal b = new BigDecimal("0.1").add(new BigDecimal("0.2"));
System.out.println(b.compareTo(new BigDecimal("0.3")) == 0); // true
```

Another mistake is forgetting edge cases: empty arrays, single-element arrays, no valid answer. Always handle these before the main logic.

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer states time and space complexity for every solution, explains why the chosen pattern is optimal, and discusses trade-offs. They handle edge cases explicitly, use money-safe types, and structure each algorithm as a self-contained class with clear single responsibility. They know when to use `ConcurrentHashMap` over `HashMap` in multi-threaded services, and they understand that Big-O hides constants that matter at scale.
